        {
          "source_quote": "μὴ ἐννοούμενον καὶ λέγοντα ἐν τῇ καρδίᾳ αὐτοῦ Χρονίζει ὁ κύριός μου ἐλθεῖν καὶ ἄρξηται τύπτειν τοὺς συνδούλους αὐτοῦ, ἐσθίων καὶ πίνων μετά τε πορνῶν καὶ μεθυόντων",
          "english_quote": "Without taking thought, he says in his heart, ‘My lord is delaying his coming,’ and begins to beat his fellow servants, eating and drinking with both prostitutes and drunkards.",
          "issue": "The draft turns accusative participles qualifying the appointed man into an independent affirmative continuation and renders subjunctive ἄρξηται as unqualified “begins.” A narrow scope for μή is a possible interpretation, so its existence alone is not a failure. Nevertheless, that scope does not itself justify the draft’s affirmative narrative or the loss of dependent/modal force. The note acknowledges the constructional shift and competing scopes but gives no contextual reason for choosing this main rendering.",
          "suggested_revision": "Keep the conduct within a qualification or warning rather than an unqualified affirmative sequel. One candidate for fresh review is: “… to give them their provisions at their proper time, without thinking and saying in his heart, ‘My lord is delaying his coming,’ and beginning to beat his fellow servants, eating and drinking with both prostitutes and drunkards.” Explain that this takes μή broadly over the coordinated conduct, fitting the characterization of the blessed appointee; preserve the narrower-scope alternative and explicitly disclose the shift to subjunctive ἄρξηται and nominative participles. Do not present the proposed revision as already accepted.",
          "severity": "major"
        }
      ]
    }
